--- body ---
## Source
2026-06-04 foundations audit (guard sweep): tests/test_selfcoherence.sh
covers only the .ergrc/AGENTS.md assets. Nothing diffs the pre-commit
hook snippet in src/go/assets/integration.md against hookBody in
src/go/install.go. Ticket 0219's exit criteria ASSUME this guard
exists ("CI self-coherence guard should catch drift") -- it does not.

## Fix
Add a coherence guard: extract the hook block from integration.md and
compare it to the hookBody constant (byte-for-byte after any declared
normalization). Failing when either side changes alone is the point.
Follow the 0146 convention: include a negative control that mutates
one side in a temp copy and asserts the guard goes red.

## Exit criteria
- [x] Guard test wired into make test (TEST_SUITES or selfcoherence)
- [x] Negative control proves the guard can fail
- [x] Guard is green against the post-0219 hook form
- [x] make check passes

## Imagine (raid 2026-06-04)
- Why now: ticket filed 2026-06-04 during audit sweep asserting guard "does not
  exist" -- but commit 473d987 (fix(0219), merged to main) already added block
  (iv) to tests/test_selfcoherence.sh (lines 82-123) covering exactly this: it
  installs the hook via `erg install --hooks`, extracts the managed block, and
  byte-compares it against the integration.md snippet between the erg managed
  markers, with a negative control proving the comparison can detect a mutation.
  The premise is stale; all four exit criteria are already satisfied on main.
- Scope check: zero new code needed -- Execute should verify the suite is green
  against the post-0219 dynamic-default-branch hookBody (criteria 3) and confirm
  `selfcoherence` is in TEST_SUITES (criteria 1, confirmed: Makefile line 18).
  Do NOT reimplement the guard; that would produce a duplicate test.
- Simplest path: Execute runs `make test-selfcoherence`, confirms PASS for all
  four assertions (clean-init, dogfood, orphan, hook-coherence + their negatives),
  ticks exit criteria, and closes 0225. No file changes beyond the ticket.
- Antipattern scan: chief risk is a tautological duplicate -- if Execute
  re-implements the guard from the ticket description without reading the file
  first, it creates redundant test code. The existing extraction strategy
  (awk over installed hook + awk over integration.md, grep -v marker lines,
  shell string compare) is the simplest correct approach for one guard; no
  general extraction framework is needed (YAGNI). Otherwise clean.
